WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
ZUCKERBERG GIVES UP:
Zuckerberg has finally given up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:09.720
the Metaverse. To most of you, this probably isn’t&nbsp;
that surprising. Within just the past two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:17.280
the Metaverse has lost Meta $24 billion.&nbsp;
But, despite the hefty losses, the memes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.400
and the lack luster adoption rates, it seemed&nbsp;
like Zuckerberg was willing to push forward no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.100
matter what. He was ready to bet a $100 billion&nbsp;
on the Metaverse over the next couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:35.400
so there was plenty of headroom left. But, it&nbsp;
seems like there was one factor that was big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.680
enough to scare him away from the Metaverse:&nbsp;
shareholders. Zuckerberg technically has over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.100
50% voting control at Meta, so he’s allowed to do&nbsp;
whatever he wants and no one can fire him. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.860
that doesn't mean that he can do whatever he wants&nbsp;
and the stock will perform well. If investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.900
don’t believe in the future of the company,&nbsp;
they’re gonna dump the stock in masses and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:05.460
exactly whats been happening at Meta. Between late&nbsp;
2021 and late 2022, Meta stock plummeted an eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:16.200
watering 77%. This translates to a market cap loss&nbsp;
of wait for it $800 billion. It looks like this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.420
exactly the reality check that Zuckerberg needed&nbsp;
as he has completely shifted his tone on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:27.120
Metaverse. Of course, he hasn’t publicly denounced&nbsp;
the Metaverse or admitted that it’s a failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.180
but actions speak louder than words. Within&nbsp;
the past 6 months, Meta has laid of a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:41.100
21,000 employees. This equates to about 25% of&nbsp;
their total workforce and I think you can guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.640
which department was affected the most. Also, the&nbsp;
layoffs may not even be done yet. There’s rumors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:54.000
that Meta may have one last round of layoffs in&nbsp;
May, so by the time you’re watching, Meta may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.760
have laid off another 5 to 10,000 employees. This&nbsp;
would effectively bring Meta’s employee count down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.880
to pre pandemic pre metaverse levels. Zuckerberg&nbsp;
has also completely stopped talking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:12.300
Metaverse. I wonder why. Unsurprisingly, this&nbsp;
has allowed Meta to destroy their recent earnings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:18.300
report and that’s despite still paying mountains&nbsp;
in severance. Within the next 6 months or so as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.400
the severance phases out, Meta will not only&nbsp;
be larger than ever but they will also be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.580
efficient than ever. Investors couldn’t ask for&nbsp;
anything more and they’ve already started to price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:35.940
in this scenario. Within just the past 6 months,&nbsp;
Meta stock has bounced back an impressive 200%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.480
This has allowed Meta to reclaim their spot as&nbsp;
a fortune 10 company and for Zuckerberg to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.880
a cool $42 billion onto his fortune just&nbsp;
this year alone. So, here’s the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.400
Zuckerberg’s insane gamble with the Metaverse and&nbsp;
how he managed to walk away stronger than ever.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.980
A FAILING CORE:&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:06.660
Taking a look back, the story of the Metaverse&nbsp;
dates back nearly 10 years at this point to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:13.380
2014. At the time, Facebook would acquire Oculus&nbsp;
for $2 billion which allowed them to take their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:19.020
first steps into the world of virtual reality.&nbsp;
Facebook wasn’t alone in this venture as a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:24.420
of big tech companies were also interested in&nbsp;
this future. Samsung and Sony were also hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.940
at work creating their own VR products, and&nbsp;
who could forget about Google Cardboard. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.860
over the years, most of these companies have&nbsp;
scaled back their VR efforts or straight up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:41.100
discontinued the projects altogether. They could&nbsp;
all see what we saw. The tech just wasn’t ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.800
for mass adoption. But, despite the early red&nbsp;
flags, Facebook would not only lean into VR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.580
but Mark would go onto to bet the entire future&nbsp;
of the company on it, so what happened? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:59.400
a lot of Mark’s choices make a lot more sense when&nbsp;
its contextualized with the state of Facebook as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:05.460
whole. By the second half of the 2010s, Zuckerberg&nbsp;
had come to the realization that social media is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:11.400
extremely fickle. There’s always a new kid on the&nbsp;
block that’s soaking up all the attention. First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:17.220
there was Friendster, then MySpace, then Facebook,&nbsp;
then Instagram, then Snapchat, then TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.040
and now potentially BeReal. So, the reality&nbsp;
was that it was just a matter of time until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.680
Facebook was replaced. At first, Facebook tried&nbsp;
to address this by just buying out the new kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:35.520
for stupid sums of money. He bought Instagram&nbsp;
for $1 billion and Whatsapp for $19 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.220
but flexing his wealth only got him so far. Pretty&nbsp;
soon, the people that he was trying to buy out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:47.640
started to reject his deals. Snapchat was likely&nbsp;
the first notable example, rejecting a $3 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:54.060
buyout from Facebook. Realizing that he couldn’t&nbsp;
just buyout everyone, Mark naturally shifted his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.060 --> 00:05:01.260
strategy to copying everyone. At the end of 2012,&nbsp;
for example, Facebook launched Poke which was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:07.860
shameless rip off of Snapchat. You probably don’t&nbsp;
remember this though as it was a massive failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.260
Learning from this Facebook would stop trying&nbsp;
to launch new apps. Instead, they would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:18.780
implement popular features into their existing&nbsp;
apps. For example, they implemented Stories and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:26.040
Reels into Instagram. But, while these features&nbsp;
were decently successful, they by no means stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:33.000
Snapchat or TikTok from succeeding. So, by the&nbsp;
late 2010s, Mark came to the realization that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.000
couldn’t just buy or copy his way into stability.&nbsp;
The only way to achieve stability was to shift to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.820
a more stable industry. His dream with Facebook&nbsp;
was always to build a social highway and to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.460
advertisers as toll booths. The hole in this&nbsp;
plan is that when it comes to social highways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:56.880
people are always moving cities. What Mark really&nbsp;
wanted was to control something like the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.620
but it was obviously impossible for Facebook&nbsp;
to takeover the internet itself in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:08.280
2010s. If they wanted to control something so&nbsp;
large, the only option was to get in early into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:15.480
the next internet. And this brings us into Mark’s&nbsp;
obsession with virtual reality or the Metaverse.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.600
A HOPELESS GAMBLE:&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:28.260
Things didn’t start off hopeless for the&nbsp;
Metaverse; in fact, they started off pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:33.960
bright actually. Oculus was by far the most&nbsp;
respected player within the VR scene and hardcore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:41.580
gamers loved their products, but there was one&nbsp;
major limiting factor: cost. Oculus headsets were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:48.240
simply unaffordable for the masses. The Oculus&nbsp;
Rift came in at $600 which in itself isn’t cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:54.660
but that was by no means the only expense. The&nbsp;
Rift was a tethered headset meaning that it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:00.180
to be connected to a PC to function. Of course,&nbsp;
you could connect the headset to your beaten up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:05.760
laptop, but if you wanted any sort of meaningful&nbsp;
VR experience, you had to buy a multi thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.400
dollar PC which just wasn’t practical for the&nbsp;
average joe. So, Zuckerberg decided to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:18.600
this concern head on with the Meta Quest 2 which&nbsp;
came in at a mere $300 to $400, no PC required.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:23.640
Suddenly, the price of VR was comparable&nbsp;
to that of a gaming console; in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:29.820
it was cheaper than most gaming consoles. The main&nbsp;
reason for this was that Meta was taking massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:36.900
losses on these units, but that was ok. They just&nbsp;
wanted everyone to buy and use the darn thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:42.720
and they could figure out how to monetize&nbsp;
later. It seemed like their plan was playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.300
out perfectly. Given the hype around VR and the&nbsp;
relatively affordable price of the Meta Quest 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:56.340
people were buying it in the masses. In fact, Meta&nbsp;
has sold nearly 20 million of these headsets, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:04.680
there was just one teensy tiny little problem. No&nbsp;
one was using the headsets. People would buy it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:12.180
try it out for a few days, and then use it as a&nbsp;
dust collector indefinitely. Why you ask? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:19.260
the biggest reason is simply that the experience&nbsp;
just sucks. The entire point of VR is to have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:25.860
immersive almost real life experience, and the&nbsp;
only way to do that is use powerful Nvidia GPUs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:32.280
paired with airplane jet engines as coolers. When&nbsp;
you try to do the same thing with an iPhone chip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:40.260
for $400, well, a 360 degree Mii world is what&nbsp;
you get. The hardware was so limited that Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:45.540
couldn’t even produce legs for your avatars&nbsp;
much less make them look realistic. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.684
they couldn’t even produce legs for demonstration&nbsp;
purposes. They would end up cheating using pre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.684 --> 00:08:57.360
rendered animations. And don’t even get me started&nbsp;
on the enterprise side of things. Mark was selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.800
the Metaverse as a way for business professionals&nbsp;
to conduct more authentic virtual meetings that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:08.340
felt like real life. Not a bad idea in theory.&nbsp;
But with graphics like this, these meetings by no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:14.520
means felt professional and geniuine. If anything,&nbsp;
they felt childish and out of place in a corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:20.880
setting, not to mention expensive. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;
virtually every company has decided to stick with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.680
Teams and Zoom. We should mention that after&nbsp;
receiving all this negative feedback about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:31.500
lack luster immersion, Meta would come out with a&nbsp;
higher end headset called the Meta Quest Pro for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:38.040
$1500. But, by the time this headset came out,&nbsp;
the pros were already out and the reputation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:44.040
the Metaverse had already been tarnished. Not to&nbsp;
mention, the pro wasn’t all the much better to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:50.100
begin with. So, lets just say, the Meta Quest&nbsp;
Pro wasn’t received very well and Meta would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:56.280
end up slashing the price by $500 just 5 months&nbsp;
after release marking the beginning of the end.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:00.960
A NEW GAMBLE:&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:08.760
There’s really no way to sugar coat it.&nbsp;
Meta is pulling back on the Metaverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.460
hard and it’s no wonder why. They’re far from&nbsp;
producing the tech and experience they want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:19.620
developing the Metaverse is taking a massive toll&nbsp;
on the company’s bottom line and stock price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:25.680
and the reputation of the Metaverse is beyond&nbsp;
repair. Now of course, if you ask Zuckerberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.440
he’ll tell you that they’re be no means ditching&nbsp;
the Metaverse, but all of the evidence is against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:37.560
this. For starters, Meta has laid off a quarter of&nbsp;
their workforce. With a median salary of $300,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:44.400
this is saving Facebook roughly $6.3 billion&nbsp;
every year. Seems counterintuitive for someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.860
who's planning on investing a $100 billion into&nbsp;
the Metaverse. But, likely the best evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:54.540
Meta is indeed moving away from the Metaverse is&nbsp;
the fact that Zuckerberg has become obsessed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.540 --> 00:11:00.900
something else: AI. According to Meta’s CTO,&nbsp;
Andrew Bosworth, Mark, Meta’s chief product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:07.320
officer, Chris Cox, and him are all spending most&nbsp;
of their time working on AI and more specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:14.340
generative AI. It seems that Mark has very much&nbsp;
gotten caught up with the ChatGPT hype and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.900
wants to get a piece of the action. Meta has&nbsp;
even announced their own large language model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:24.240
called LLaMa and they’re currently working on&nbsp;
releasing this to researchers. Bosworth has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.100
also confirmed that they’re working on releasing&nbsp;
a commercial AI product by the end of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:35.880
All of this may be a bit confusing. It seemed like&nbsp;
Zuckerberg was super gung ho about the Metaverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:42.900
so how did he pivot so quickly? Well, the reality&nbsp;
is simply that Meta’s commitment to the Metaverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:49.200
was simply overhyped by the media. In fact, the&nbsp;
Metaverse wasn’t even Zuckerberg’s first attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:54.360
to pivot Facebook away from social media.&nbsp;
Before he got obsessed with VR, Zuckerberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:59.760
was actually trying to build a space business. He&nbsp;
was launching a bunch of solar powered drones and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.680
atmospheric satellites with the goal of trying&nbsp;
to create satellite based internet similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:10.500
Starlink today. But, as he saw that this wasn’t&nbsp;
picking up any steam and that SpaceX was gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:16.020
do it themselves, Zuckerberg didn’t hesitate to&nbsp;
kill off the program completely and switch to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:22.080
Metaverse. So, who’s to say that Zuckerberg isn’t&nbsp;
willing to do the same thing with the Metaverse.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.700
THE TRUTH ABOUT THE METAVERSE:
Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:34.740
that the Metaverse was much more consumer&nbsp;
oriented and futuristic than their space plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:40.920
it garnered a lot more media attention, but for&nbsp;
Facebook it was just another attempt to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:47.580
free from social media. By now though, it’s become&nbsp;
clear that this path is not so viable and they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.380
accordingly pulling back substantially.&nbsp;
This isn’t to say that Meta is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:57.960
ditching the Metaverse quite yet. They’re still&nbsp;
pouring resources into the idea but they're by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:03.000
no means adamant to sticking to just that. If&nbsp;
they can get something else to work instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:10.440
great! In the end, the bottomline is that Meta&nbsp;
has become a mature social media company meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:16.080
that they only have two paths forward.&nbsp;
They can either go all in on social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:23.160
optimize their operations, and become a dividend&nbsp;
giant. Or, they can try to hit the next big thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:27.720
With his performance over the past 6&nbsp;
months, Zuckerberg has made it clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:32.940
that he can achieve the former at any time&nbsp;
within a matter of 12 months if he wants to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:40.260
But, what he really wants is the latter. He wants&nbsp;
is to grow Meta to the next magnitude and the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:46.140
way to do that is to hit a grand slam. He took a&nbsp;
few swings at the Metaverse and it looks like he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:51.960
struck out. But, being only 38 years old with&nbsp;
majority control over the 8th largest company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:59.040
in the world, I assure you, he has a lot of swings&nbsp;
left. So, while people love mocking the Metaverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:05.280
it’s Zuckerberg that’s getting the last laugh.&nbsp;
Are you a fan of the Metaverse? Comment that down&nbsp;&nbsp;

